<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Key insight</strong>: cl=
ientLeft is not just border width - it's "distance from element's left edge=
 to content area start". This distinction becomes crucial in RTL layouts.</=
p>
<hr class=3D"border-border-300 my-2">
<h3 class=3D"text-lg font-bold text-text-100 mt-1 -mb-1.5">=F0=9F=93=96 cli=
entWidth/clientHeight: Content Area Dimensions</h3>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">=F0=9F=8C=B1 <strong>Ngu=E1=BB=
=93n G=E1=BB=91c &amp; Motivation:</strong></p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">clientWidth/clientHeight solve m=
=E1=BB=99t fundamental problem trong dynamic layout: "How much space is act=
ually available for content?"</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">This is different from offsetWid=
th/offsetHeight b=E1=BB=9Fi v=C3=AC:</p>
<ul class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">offsetWidth includes borders</l=
i>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">clientWidth excludes borders bu=
t includes padding</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">clientWidth excludes scrollbars=
 (when they take space from content)</li>
</ul>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">These properties answer: "If I p=
lace content inside this element, how much space do I have?"</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">=F0=9F=94=AC <strong>B=E1=BA=A3n=
 Ch=E1=BA=A5t &amp; Mechanism:</strong></p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">clientWidth/clientHeight represe=
nt "padding box" minus scrollbars:</p>
